title: rotate rewrites a JSON number wider than int64 as a JSON string
updated: 2026-08-21T03:24:37Z
---

Found while measuring karr #58 (sops 3.13.3). Not a MAC failure and not data loss in the digits -- silent TYPE drift on a document the real sops wrote.

Every JSON decoder measured (Cpanel::JSON::XS 4.40, JSON::XS 4.04, JSON::PP 4.16) returns a bare PV STRING, not a number, for a JSON integer literal outside the int64 range. YAML::XS returns a float (-NP) for the same digits.

Measured end to end:

  sops -e writes    "big_unencrypted": 100000000000000000000    (a JSON number)
  File::SOPS decrypt      OK
  File::SOPS rotate       OK
  after rotate      "big_unencrypted" : "100000000000000000000"  (a JSON string)
  sops -d after rotate    exit 0

The MAC survives because the digits are the same either way -- value_to_bytes writes a string verbatim, and Go ToBytes of the parsed value gives the same text -- so nothing complains. But rotating a file has changed its schema, and anything downstream that type-checks the document now sees a string.

assert_representable does not fire because the leaf is a PV, not an IOK scalar, so the int64 guard never looks at it. Whether the right answer is to refuse the document, to preserve the number-ness, or to document the drift is open. Note that preserving number-ness is the same wrapper machinery #58 option (a) needs for floats, so it may come nearly free once that lands.
